[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://book.humanexperiences.io/", :title "Show HN: Building an LLM from Scratch in TypeScript", :link "https://book.humanexperiences.io/", :published-at #inst "2026-10-14T10:29:56.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "Show HN: Building an LLM from Scratch in TypeScript", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn-frontpage", :item-id "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50084969", :title "Ask HN: What is the current trajectory of a senior engineer?", :link "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50084969", :published-at #inst "2026-10-14T09:54:23.000-00:00", :content "Been recently thinking about this more and more: When the need for a human in the design loop ceases? Given that LLMs are RL'd to the max with everything that can be given a wrong/right answer (code/maths), what currently is stopping Anthropic, OpenAI et al. from internally building (and maybe buying) datasets of good and bad architecture, and RL'ing that? Given that LLMs were a fancy autocomplete back in 2024, and now people are talking about building \"software factories\" with the agents, what in your mind is the stop to this development? Why software architecture would be hard for agents still in a year or two? Just thinking because I see more and more examples of software developers ignoring this and saying \"humans still have a place in guiding the agents and designing the architecture\". I think (businesswise) the need for actual software programmer skills is 90% out in a few years I think, and product lead etc people will be favored more as they have more people skills. it was a good run. Comments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50084969 Points: 25 # Comments: 32"}, :excerpts [{:text "... a human in the design loop ceases? Given that LLMs are RL'd to the max with everything that can be...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... bad architecture, and RL'ing that? Given that LLMs were a fancy autocomplete back in 2024, and now...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://technology.blog.gov.uk/?p=5522", :title "Using AI to analyse legacy systems faster: what our pilots revealed", :link "https://technology.blog.gov.uk/2026/10/14/using-ai-to-analyse-legacy-systems-faster-what-our-pilots-revealed/", :published-at #inst "2026-10-14T08:32:02.000-00:00", :content "AI can help organisations analyse legacy systems faster One of the biggest barriers to modernising legacy technology is understanding how existing systems work. To modernise systems, organisations first need a clear picture of how applications work, connect and depend on one another. That process can take weeks or even months. Documentation is often incomplete or out of date, knowledge may be concentrated in a small number of specialists, and some systems rely on technologies that few engineers still work with regularly. Our pilots indicate that AI-assisted approaches could help technical teams analyse some legacy systems more quickly. Across participating organisations, activities estimated to take weeks using traditional approaches were completed in hours. Working with organisations including the Student Loans Company (SLC), the UK Hydrographic Office (UKHO), the Met Office, and our delivery partner Kainos, we found that AI can help teams analyse unfamiliar systems, identify dependencies and build technical knowledge more quickly than traditional approaches alone. The pilots covered different systems and technical environments, so the results should not be treated as directly applicable to every legacy estate. However, for organisations managing large and complex technology estates, this could make some modernisation activities easier to prioritise, plan and deliver. What happened at the Student Loans Company At the Student Loans Company, teams explored how AI assisted approaches could be used to analyse an estate of 33 applications written in Clojure and support future modernisation planning. Using traditional approaches, the work was estimated to take around five weeks. During the pilot, teams completed the analysis in less than a day. The analysis gave the team a clearer view of the 33 applications and their dependencies, which it used to inform migration planning and work towards supported versions of Java. The most significant finding was not the reduction in effort itself, but the ability to unlock work that had previously been difficult to prioritise. What happened at the UK Hydrographic Office At the UK Hydrographic Office, teams focused on a 20-year old desktop application that previously had no automated tests. AI assisted techniques were used to generate automated tests for the application. Test coverage increased from 0% to 96%, while regression testing that had previously required around 15 days of manual effort was reduced to approximately 30 minutes. The benefits extended beyond faster testing. Teams reported greater confidence in making changes to the application safely and maintaining it over time. For many legacy systems, the perceived risk of change can be a barrier to improvement. The pilot indicated that AI-assisted testing could give engineers more evidence when assessing proposed changes. Why this matters The examples from SLC and UKHO illustrate a wider challenge facing organisations across the public sector. Legacy systems often support critical services, but analysing them can be complex and expensive. Teams may spend weeks mapping dependencies, reviewing code and building technical knowledge before they can assess risks or plan change. The strongest finding from the pilots was that AI can help accelerate this discovery and assessment phase. That may sound modest, but it addresses a critical point where many modernisation efforts slow down or stall. By helping teams understand systems more quickly, AI assisted approaches can make it easier to: assess risks identify system dependencies prioritise investment improve technical documentation develop modernisation plans In short, AI can help organisations spend less time understanding their systems and more time improving them. AI is not a substitute for good delivery The pilots also reinforced an important lesson. AI does not remove the need for good governance, engineering expertise or delivery capability. Successful outcomes depended on organisations having the right foundations in place, including clear ownership, access to systems and information, experienced technical teams and established delivery processes. In practice, the pilots showed that AI amplified existing strengths and weaknesses rather than replacing them. Used well, AI can accelerate analysis, improve visibility of complex systems and help organisations make progress more quickly. Used without appropriate controls, it can amplify existing problems and accelerate poor decision making. Where AI-assisted analysis may help The pilots suggest that AI can significantly reduce the effort required to assess complex systems, helping organisations move more quickly from discovery to modernisation. For organisations managing large and complex technology estates, this can help engineers, architects and delivery teams to identify risks earlier, making modernisation activities easier to plan, justify and deliver. What happens next We are continuing to work with organisations using AI assisted approaches to analyse, assess and modernise legacy systems, building on the lessons from these pilots. Alongside this work, we are developing a playbook of good practice that captures practical approaches, lessons learned and implementation considerations for organisations looking to apply AI to legacy technology challenges. The playbook is currently undergoing user testing, review and approval ahead of publication. Get involved If your organisation is exploring AI assisted approaches to analysing, documenting or modernising legacy systems, or would like to share experiences and engage with our wider Firmer Foundations programme, we would like to hear from you. Get in touch at firmer.foundations@dsit.gov.uk"}, :excerpts [{:text "... analyse an estate of 33 applications written in Clojure and support future modernisation planning. Using...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... planning and work towards supported versions of Java. The most significant finding was not the...", :matched-terms ["java"], :source :content} {:text "... existing strengths and weaknesses rather than replacing them. Used well, AI can accelerate analysis,...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://dev.to/ohugonnot/best-clean-code-and-refactoring-books-and-the-order-to-read-them-2c24", :title "Best Clean Code and Refactoring Books, and the Order to Read Them", :link "https://dev.to/ohugonnot/best-clean-code-and-refactoring-books-and-the-order-to-read-them-2c24", :published-at #inst "2026-10-14T09:00:02.000-00:00", :content "Clean Code was the first text I handed to my students. Since then I have read the six other books fighting for the \"clean code\" shelf, and they do not all say the same thing. Sometimes they say the opposite. I wrote a reading note for each one. Here is the order I would follow if I were starting over, and what you can leave on the shelf. The craft before the rules The Pragmatic Programmer by Thomas and Hunt (2019 edition, 8/10) is my way in. What strikes you first is the tone: few commandments, many stories, never a condescending look. And this edition publicly corrects the previous one. The Law of Demeter is demoted, DRY (do not duplicate a piece of knowledge) is clarified because people misread it, and on security the authors write \"we were wrong\". A book that owns its mistakes twenty years later earns trust for everything else. The limit is mechanical: 53 topics in 352 pages means skimming. The tooling chapter is thin, and the code samples jump from Ruby to Elixir to Clojure without digging into any of them. You read it for the stories and mental models, not the code. Skip the samples, keep the rest. The dogma and its counterweight Clean Code by Robert C. Martin (2008, 6.8/10) taught a generation that code is read ten times more than it is written. The chapters on naming, functions and comments have not aged a day. The rest is verbose Java, and the last three case-study chapters are 90 pages of listings nobody reads anymore, me included. One thing the fans forget: Martin himself admits that no research backs the short-functions rule, and that his own functions start out long and messy before being refined under tests. The \"three lines maximum\" dogma comes more from his readers than from the book. To hear the other bell, read A Philosophy of Software Design by John Ousterhout (2021, 8/10). A hundred and ninety-six pages, and in my eyes the best software design book of the last twenty years. His deep module idea changed how I judge a class: I stopped counting lines and started comparing what the interface costs to learn with what it gives back. The examples circle around two or three cases (a text editor, RAMCloud) and smell like a lecture hall. And \"make your modules deep\" is easier to understand than to apply. Where the two books contradict each other, on function size and on comments, Ousterhout is almost always right. The first hundred pages of construction Code Complete by Steve McConnell (2004, 960 pages, 7.4/10) is the book I wish I had read at the start of my career, but for its first hundred pages, not its 960. The thesis on complexity, abstract data types, cohesive routines, defensive barricades: you usually learn all of that by trial and error, after humbling code reviews. The book shortcuts that painful education. What has aged is visible: samples in C++, Visual Basic 6 and Pascal, \"code tuning\" chapters about processor pipelines, a tools catalogue straight from a museum. What holds up is everything about humans: the numbers on code inspections, the psychology of debugging, complexity as the universal enemy. You do not need all of it. Parts I, II and VII plus chapters 20 and 21 make roughly 250 pages, and the rest is reference material you open now and then. Refactoring and legacy, the two moves Refactoring by Martin Fowler (2018, 8.2/10) holds the best code lesson I have ever read. Chapter 1 is seventy pages of watching someone work, every move explained, the code never broken. Fowler has a rare honesty: his central argument is labeled \"unproven hypothesis\". The limit is that half the book is a dictionary of transformations nobody reads in one sitting. Read chapters 1 to 4 like a novel and keep the rest within reach during code review. The Refactor menu in your editor exists because of this book. Then comes Working Effectively with Legacy Code by Michael Feathers (2004, 7/10), which redefines the word: legacy is not old code, it is code without tests. His characterization tests (which snapshot current behavior) answer a question nobody phrases well: how do you start testing when you do not know what \"correct\" means for this code? The seam model (places where you can change behavior without editing the code) teaches you to read code by looking for joints. Feathers also admits that his techniques often make code uglier in the short term, and that the scar is temporary. That frankness makes the book reliable. It is dated, and every example is Java, C++ or C. Part II, twenty chapters in FAQ format, often repeats the same four moves under a different class name. Open it at the chapter matching your problem of the day. Test first, to lose the fear Test-Driven Development: By Example by Kent Beck (2002, 240 pages, 7.6/10) does not sell a recipe, it shows a state of mind: the calm of knowing, every second, that the code still works. The fear of breaking things turned into quiet boredom. Since then I no longer write tests to \"have coverage\", I write them to move forward without dread. Part I, the money example, drags: you repeat the same micro-cycle dozens of times and you got it long before. The code is 2002 Java and Python 2. TDD is still debated, and Beck is the first to say no study settles it. Read it for the mindset, keep the cycle, the TODO list and \"fake it\" when you are stuck, and adapt the micro-steps to your own pace. The order I would follow Book Pages Rating What it is for 1 The Pragmatic Programmer 352 8 The craft's mental models 2 Clean Code 464 6.8 Naming, functions, comments 3 A Philosophy of Software Design 196 8 The counterweight: deep modules 4 Refactoring 448 8.2 Chapters 1 to 4, then the catalog as needed 5 TDD by Example 240 7.6 Moving forward without fear of breaking 6 Working Effectively with Legacy Code 440 7 Testing code that never had tests as needed Code Complete 960 7.4 The useful 250 pages, then reference Conclusion The book I used to hand my students has the lowest rating on the list, 6.8/10. And it is Fowler and Feathers who hold the moves you actually use on a Monday morning, in code you did not write. If you want to see these moves applied to a real project, I wrote about the order of a legacy Symfony audit. I stitched the marrow of dozens of tech books into a single thread, from bits and Big O up to the AI era: that is The Developer's Great Book. Four of these books (Clean Code, A Philosophy of Software Design, The Pragmatic Programmer, Refactoring) are melted into it at the \"Writing for humans\" level, and three others (TDD by Example, Working Effectively with Legacy Code, Code Complete) at the \"Prove it, ship it, hold the load\" level. 📚 Other comparisons. Best DevOps and Engineering Team Books, and the Order to Read Them and Best Event Sourcing and CQRS Books, and the Order to Read Them."}, :excerpts [{:text "... way in. What strikes you first is the tone: few commandments, many stories, never a condescending look....", :matched-terms ["command"], :source :content} {:text "... and the code samples jump from Ruby to Elixir to Clojure without digging into any of them. You read it for...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... have not aged a day. The rest is verbose Java, and the last three case-study chapters are 90...", :matched-terms ["java"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-14T10:47:32.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-14T10:47:32.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]}]